**Example 5.** Compute \(E_{n}(x)\), _n_=1(1)_N_, to 5S for _x_=10, _N_=10.

If, as in this example, \(x\) is appreciably larger than five and _N_\(\leq\)_x_, then the recurrence relation 5.I.14 may be safely used in decreasing order of _n_([5.5]). From Table 5.5 for _x_-1=.1 we get \((x^{+}_{-}10)e^{z}E_{10}(x)\)=1.02436 so that \(E_{10}(10)\)=2.32529 \(\times 10^{-6}\). Using this as the initial value we obtain column (2).



From Table 5.2 we get \(x\!e^{x}E_{1}(x)\!=\!.915633\) so that \(E_{1}(10)\!=\!4.15697\!\times\!10^{-6}\) as a check. Forward recurrence starting with \(E_{1}(10)\!=\!4.1570\!\times\!10^{-6}\) yields the values in column (1). The underlined figures are in error.

